  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nWe propose a sequential convex optimization approach using the SLSQP algorithm to minimize the maximum overlap integral, leveraging the initial solution as a starting point. The strategy uses gradient-based optimization combined with careful constraint handling to explore the solution landscape effectively.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\n- Decision variable: h \u2208 \u211d^n, where n = n_points.\n- Objective: minimize C5 = max_k (sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i](1 - h[(i+k) mod n)) * dx.\n- Constraints: h[i] \u2208 [0,1] \u2200i, sum(h) = n / 2.\n- The optimizer seeks the h that minimizes the maximum overlap across all shifts.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na) Initialization:\n1. Read initial_h_values.\n2. Compute n_points and dx.\n3. Set initial guess h0 = initial_h_values.\n\nb) Optimization loop:\n1. Use scipy.optimize.minimize with SLSQP solver.\n   - Objective function: compute C5.\n   - Bounds: (0,1) for each h[i].\n   - Equality constraint: sum(h) = n / 2.\n2. Every 100 seconds, perturb h slightly and restart.\n\nc) Termination:\n1. Stop when time budget is exhausted.\n2. Final projection to ensure feasibility.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- Initial guess: Use initial_h_values.\n- Perturbation: Add 0.01 * random_normal to h.\n- Solver settings: tolerance=1e-6, maxiter=1000.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n- Each SLSQP iteration: ~10-100s.\n- Perturbations ensure robust exploration.\n- Final projection ensures feasibility.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n- Verify sum(h) = n / 2 and h \u2208 [0,1].\n- Compute C5 via evaluator.\n- Return h, C5, and n_points.",
